Verdict: PASS. I wrote it to session_plan/eval_task_2.md. I judged from the diff, a few targeted reads, and the harness's passing build and tests. I didn't run the binary myself; the six new tests do run it against a local stub.

• Implementation: the file-writing check that emit_output used is now its own function, write_output_for_outcome. Both stream-json branches call it through a new stream_json_exit_code: the -p path in run_single_prompt and the piped-stdin path in run_piped_mode. It applies the same failure rule (run_failed_overall) as the other modes, so the logic isn't copied. The "wrote / replaced / was not written" lines go to stderr, so stdout stays a plain NDJSON event stream.
• Tests: six new tests in tests/output_failed_run.rs cover:
  • a successful run creating the file;
  • a successful run replacing an existing file;
  • a refusal leaving the file byte-identical, exit 1;
  • an HTTP 400 creating no file, exit non-zero;
  • Ctrl+C keeping the file, exit 130;
  • the piped-stdin path writing the file.

  Each success and refusal test also checks that every stdout line parses as JSON.
• One change outside the listed files: src/prompt.rs gets one line that records the Ctrl+C in stream-json mode. Without it the interrupted run couldn't be told apart from a finished one. The task needed it, but note that Ctrl+C under stream-json now exits 130 instead of 0, the same as -p already does.
• Docs: the --output paragraph in docs/src/usage/piped-mode.md now covers stream-json. It also says that SIGTERM, SIGHUP and SIGQUIT leave the file untouched under stream-json but print no note.
• Product surface: no defaults, flags or setup behaviour changed, so the opt-in rule from #448 doesn't apply.

I didn't check the commit message body (before/after rows, recall control) or whether the result was posted on #1219.
